        "id": 1140964
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-27T12:36:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Apparently not. Textures are \"virtual\" objects, they are stored wherever.\nWhat is possible is to write to a Surface (like done before), and then copy the surface to a texture. The result is the same as what is done here.\n\nI followed the following to migrate https://wiki.libsdl.org/SDL2/MigrationGuide:\n\nAt this point, your 1.2 game had a bunch of SDL_Surfaces, which it would SDL_BlitSurface() to the screen surface to compose the final framebuffer, and eventually SDL_Flip() to the screen. For SDL 2.0, you have a bunch of SDL_Textures, that you will SDL_RenderCopy() to your Renderer to compose the final framebuffer, and eventually SDL_RenderPresent() to the screen. It\u0027s that simple. If these textures never need modification, you might find your framerate has just gone through the roof, too.",
      "parentUuid": "3a77816f_67777674",
      "revId": "a7ec221283bd972f97e2c6cb366898826b5f2b6b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
